<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    FusionRpg.Bridge — the shared surface between the engine core and the game host.

    WHY THIS ASSEMBLY EXISTS. Three Unity-free types — ActorHudCache, ActorHudUniqueFlags and
    InjectorDerivedOverride — are per-pointer state that BOTH sides must agree on: the core's HUD
    and status logic reads it, and the host's observe, capture and stat-apply paths write it. They
    lived in the host repository (gk-fusion) purely because of where their NAMESPACE read best,
    not because they needed a game. Three gk-core test projects therefore compiled them in through
    `$(GkFusionRoot)`, which meant a standalone clone of gk-core could not build at all — 12 CS2001
    "source file not found" errors, measured — while gk-core's own AGENTS.md says plainly:

        Never compile against gk-forge or gk-fusion.

    So the compile-link contradicted the rule that gives the rule its reason. This module is the
    third option, and the only one that satisfies the rule, the build and the test at once.

    THE NAMING RULE, and it is not cosmetic. Nothing here may be named after the game it happens
    to serve. These types know about pointers, HUD snapshots and derived-stat overrides; they know
    nothing about plants, zombies, or which loader host is running. A name carrying the game's name
    would make a shared contract look like host code, and the next person would move it back - and
    the split would break again in the same place. The repo's own vocabulary already spells this
    "creature" rather than "zombie" for exactly this reason, and the rule is stated in AGENTS.md.

    DEPENDENCY DIRECTION. This assembly references FusionRpg.Core and nothing else, so it can be
    referenced by FusionRpg.Core's own test projects (as it is) and by the host (as it will be)
    without either compiling the other. It does NOT reference FusionRpg.Contracts directly: Core
    already brings it, and a second edge would be a cycle waiting to happen.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- net6.0, NOT net8.0, and the reason is a consumer rather than a preference.
         FusionRpg.Core is net6.0 and every loader host in gk-fusion - BepInEx, MelonLoader,
         MelonLoader.39 and MelonLoader.40 - is net6.0, because the game host they inject into
         loads on .NET 6. A net8.0-only module here is not "newer", it is INCOMPATIBLE with every
         consumer this module exists to serve: restore fails with NU1201 and gk-fusion does not
         build at all. gk-core's own net8.0 test projects can still reference it, because a net8.0
         project consumes a net6.0 library without complaint - the asymmetry runs one way only.

         This was shipped wrong once, as net8.0, and the mistake survived a recorded "0 errors"
         verification claim because the check counted `error CS\d+` and cannot see `error NU\d+`.
         A restore error is not a compile error, and an instrument that only counts compile errors
         reports a broken build as a clean one. The comment is here so the next person does not
         "upgrade" it and reintroduce NU1201. -->
    <TargetFramework>net6.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <RootNamespace>FusionRpg.Bridge</RootNamespace>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>AGPL-3.0-or-later</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <!-- This module is the shared contract, so it must never reach for Unity, the loader, or a
         game assembly. The guard is a build error rather than a review comment, because a guard
         that only warns is a guard that gets ignored. -->
    <EnableDefaultCompileItems>true</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\FusionRpg.Core\FusionRpg.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
